      "content": "Crucial to the Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle is the concept of a wave packet, a superposition of waves with different wavelengths and frequencies that, together, embody the wave-like properties of a particle. In Quantum Mechanics, particles are not localized to precise points but instead exist within a probability wave function, Ψ(x,t), which describes the quantum state of a particle in terms of its position x and time t. This wave function contains all the measurable information about the system.\n\nWhen we make an attempt to measure the position of a particle, we essentially collapse the wave function to a smaller region. The more localized the wave function is in space, the greater the spread in momentum space; hence, a greater uncertainty in momentum. Mathematically, this spreading can be depicted through Fourier transforms, which link position and momentum space representations.\n\nFor a free particle, the wave function can be represented by:\n\nΨ(x,t) = A * exp[i(kx - ωt)]\n\nIn its momentum state, the Fourier transform of this wave function ∼ Ψ(p) = B * exp[-ip^2/(2mħ)], where B is a normalization constant and m is the mass of the particle, depicts the momentum distribution. The spread Δ",
